# SQL Fundamentals: Selecting, Filtering, and Joining

> SQL is the one language every relational database speaks. This
> lesson walks through SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, aggregates, and the
> JOIN family using three tiny tables you can hold in your head — and
> check every result row by hand.

## 1. One Language, Five Jobs

[definition]
SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language of relational
databases, divided by purpose into sublanguages: DDL defines
structures, DML changes data, DQL queries it, DCL controls access, and
TCL manages transactions.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="sql-sublanguages" layout="tree" aria="SQL sublanguages and their commands"
  SQL -> "DDL: define"
  SQL -> "DML: change"
  SQL -> "DQL: query"
  SQL -> "DCL: guard"
  SQL -> "TCL: transact"
  "DDL: define" -> "CREATE, ALTER, DROP"
  "DML: change" -> "INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE"
  "DQL: query" -> SELECT
  "DCL: guard" -> "GRANT, REVOKE"
  "TCL: transact" -> "COMMIT, ROLLBACK"
@/diagram

A SELECT statement has a fixed clause order — and, famously, a
different *logical evaluation* order:

| Written order | Clause | Job | Evaluated |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | SELECT | choose output columns | 5th |
| 2 | FROM | name the source tables | 1st |
| 3 | WHERE | filter rows | 2nd |
| 4 | GROUP BY | form groups | 3rd |
| 5 | HAVING | filter groups | 4th |
| 6 | ORDER BY | sort the result | 6th |
| 7 | LIMIT | keep the first few rows | 7th |

## 2. The Running Example: Three Small Tables

Everything below runs against these tables; every claimed result can
be verified by eye.

**student** — five rows:

| student_id | name | city | year |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Aisha | Delhi | 2 |
| 2 | Boris | Mumbai | 1 |
| 3 | Chen | Delhi | 3 |
| 4 | Divya | Chennai | 2 |
| 5 | Eshan | Chennai | 1 |

**course** — four rows:

| course_id | title | credits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C1 | Databases | 4 |
| C2 | Networks | 3 |
| C3 | AI | 4 |
| C4 | Compilers | 3 |

**enrolment** — five rows:

| student_id | course_id | grade |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | C1 | A |
| 1 | C2 | B |
| 2 | C1 | B |
| 3 | C1 | A |
| 4 | C3 | B |

[note]
Two deliberate gaps power the join examples later: Eshan is enrolled
in nothing, and Compilers (C4) has no students. Watch what each join
does with them.
[/note]

## 3. SELECT: Choosing Columns

Projection picks *columns* from a table:

```sql
SELECT name, city FROM student;

SELECT DISTINCT city FROM student;      -- Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai

SELECT name AS student_name,
       year  AS batch                   -- rename output headers
FROM student;
```

SELECT can also compute: `SELECT name, year + 1 AS next_year FROM
student;` produces a derived column without storing anything new.
DISTINCT collapses duplicates — with five students in three cities it
returns three rows, because it answers "which cities exist" rather
than "who lives where".

## 4. WHERE: Choosing Rows

| Operator | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| =, <>, <, >, <=, >= | comparison | year >= 2 |
| BETWEEN ... AND | inclusive range | credits BETWEEN 3 AND 4 |
| IN (...) | membership in a list | city IN ('Delhi', 'Mumbai') |
| LIKE | pattern match; % = any run, _ = one char | name LIKE 'A%' |
| IS NULL | test for missing value | city IS NULL |
| AND, OR, NOT | combine predicates | year = 2 AND city = 'Delhi' |

```sql
SELECT name FROM student
WHERE year = 2
  AND (city = 'Delhi' OR city = 'Mumbai');    -- Aisha

SELECT title FROM course
WHERE title LIKE '%s';                        -- Databases, Networks

SELECT name FROM student
WHERE city IS NULL;                           -- nobody here
```

[warning]
Never write `city = NULL`. Comparisons with NULL yield *unknown*, not
true, so the row is filtered out — the only correct test is IS NULL.
Likewise `NOT IN` with a NULL in the list makes every row fail, a
classic silent-wrong-answer bug.
[/warning]

## 5. ORDER BY, LIMIT, and the Behaviour of NULLs

```sql
SELECT name, city, year FROM student
ORDER BY city ASC, year DESC;
-- Chennai: Divya(2), Eshan(1); Delhi: Chen(3), Aisha(2); Mumbai: Boris

SELECT name, year FROM student
ORDER BY year DESC
LIMIT 2 OFFSET 1;         -- skip the top student, keep the next two
```

Multiple sort keys apply left to right: ties in city are broken by
year descending. LIMIT plus OFFSET is the backbone of pagination,
though deep offsets get slow because the engine still has to produce
and discard the skipped rows.

NULLs sort position is engine-specific — PostgreSQL puts them last
ascending by default, MySQL first — so order-critical queries say
`NULLS LAST` (or equivalent) explicitly.

## 6. Aggregates, GROUP BY, and HAVING

Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value: COUNT, SUM,
AVG, MIN, MAX.

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*)        AS total,
       COUNT(DISTINCT city) AS cities,
       MIN(year)       AS youngest_batch
FROM student;
-- 5, 3, 1
```

GROUP BY partitions rows into buckets and aggregates per bucket.
Walk it in three steps for `SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS students FROM
student GROUP BY city;`:

1. Partition the five rows: Delhi gets Aisha and Chen, Mumbai gets
   Boris, Chennai gets Divya and Eshan.
2. Collapse each bucket to one row.
3. Output: Delhi 2, Mumbai 1, Chennai 2.

WHERE filters *rows before* grouping; HAVING filters *groups after*:

```sql
SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS students
FROM student
WHERE year >= 2          -- drops Boris and Eshan
GROUP BY city
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2;    -- keeps Delhi only
```

[warning]
Two classic errors: putting a column in SELECT that is neither grouped
nor aggregated (`SELECT city, name, COUNT(*) ...` fails), and writing
`WHERE COUNT(*) >= 2` — aggregates are not allowed in WHERE; use
HAVING. Remember: COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(col) skips NULLs in that
column.
[/warning]

## 7. JOINs: Putting Tables Back Together

Normalisation splits tables to remove redundancy; joins recombine them
at query time. All joins pair rows across a shared key:

```sql
SELECT s.name, e.course_id, e.grade
FROM student s
INNER JOIN enrolment e ON s.student_id = e.student_id;
-- Aisha C1 A; Aisha C2 B; Boris C1 B; Chen C1 A; Divya C3 B
```

An INNER JOIN keeps only matched pairs. Outer joins keep unmatched
rows too, padded with NULLs — this is where Eshan and Compilers
reappear:

```sql
SELECT s.name, e.course_id
FROM student s
LEFT JOIN enrolment e ON s.student_id = e.student_id;
-- the five pairs above, plus: Eshan NULL
```

Which join to reach for is a two-question decision:

@diagram flowchart id="which-join" layout="hierarchical" aria="Deciding between join types"
node start label="Two tables share a key" shape="pill"
node d1 label="Keep left rows with no match?" shape="diamond"
node d2 label="Keep right rows with no match?" shape="diamond"
node inner label="INNER JOIN" shape="round-rect"
node left label="LEFT JOIN" shape="round-rect"
node full label="FULL OUTER JOIN" shape="round-rect"

start -> d1
d1 -> d2 label="no"
d1 -> left label="yes"
d2 -> inner label="no"
d2 -> full label="yes"
@/diagram

Counting result rows on the sample data makes the differences vivid:

@chart bar title="Rows returned by each join on the sample data" labels:
INNER
LEFT (student to enrolment)
RIGHT (student to enrolment)
FULL OUTER
CROSS

data:
5
6
5
6
20
@/chart

[tip]
A CROSS JOIN (no ON condition) pairs every row with every row — 5
students times 4 courses is 20 rows. Forgetting your ON clause
silently turns an inner join into a cross join; a 5-row answer
suddenly becoming 20 is the tell-tale symptom.
[/tip]

A table can join to itself — find pairs of students sharing a city:

```sql
SELECT a.name, b.name
FROM student a
JOIN student b
  ON a.city = b.city AND a.student_id < b.student_id;
-- Aisha + Chen (Delhi), Divya + Eshan (Chennai)
```

## 8. Subqueries, Set Operations, and Style

A subquery is a query nested inside another — most often inside WHERE:

```sql
SELECT name FROM student
WHERE student_id IN (SELECT student_id
                     FROM enrolment
                     WHERE course_id = 'C1');   -- Aisha, Boris, Chen

SELECT title, credits FROM course
WHERE credits > (SELECT AVG(credits) FROM course);  -- above 3.5
```

Set operations combine whole results, not rows: UNION (merge, distinct),
UNION ALL (merge, keep duplicates — faster), INTERSECT, and EXCEPT
(rows in the first but not the second). Both sides must return the
same number of compatible columns.

[important]
Three habits prevent most beginner SQL bugs: qualify every column
with its table alias when more than one table is involved; test every
predicate against NULL behaviour, not just the happy path; and when a
row count surprises you, re-derive it by hand on a small table — the
sample data above exists precisely for that.
[/important]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Clause order | SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY, LIMIT |
| Evaluation order | FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY |
| DISTINCT | collapse duplicate output rows |
| WHERE vs HAVING | filter rows before grouping; filter groups after |
| Aggregates | COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(col) skips NULLs |
| INNER JOIN | matched pairs only |
| LEFT JOIN | plus unmatched left rows padded with NULLs |
| CROSS JOIN | every pair; usually an accidental missing ON |
| Set operations | UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT, EXCEPT |
| NULL rules | IS NULL to test; beware NOT IN with NULLs |

[summary]
SELECT projects columns, WHERE filters rows, GROUP BY plus aggregates
summarise buckets, and HAVING filters those buckets — evaluated FROM
first, SELECT late. Joins rebuild relationships: INNER keeps matches,
LEFT and RIGHT keep one side's strays, FULL keeps both, CROSS pairs
everything. Subqueries and set operations compose results, and the
fastest debugger is a five-row sample table checked by hand.
[/summary]
